      "math_academy_id": "q-28595",
      "knowledge_point_id": "b4b5807f-a10a-518a-84fa-907bf699884c",
      "knowledge_point": "Exponential Functions With Vertical Reflections and Horizontal Shifts",
      "problem": "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-{e}^{x}+5?$",
      "worked_solution": "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y={e}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/f5983db4731c3fde0899ffc7afd602c7c82502f581742b62e69e8e6cf2d99e93.png)\n\n \n\n- Reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-{e}^{x}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,-1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/844a32e9d3640f9244b867c7411241171902c848defa0bf653b3820c3850238d.png)\n\n \n\n- Then, vertically shift it by $5$ units up to give $y=-{e}^{x}+5.$ The point $(0,-1)$ moves to $(0,4)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from from $y=0$ to $y=5.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/c4cd8597702179629617890fce31272aa447a3227b74231e787a61eb4e770589.png)",
