    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-117799",
      "knowledge_point_id": "dac9c72b-c23f-56fa-b939-6e3b56d3a13b",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying the Horizontal Stretch and Vertical Shift of a Transformed Cotangent Curve",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/c9ed24a7fa07d6c29e4125c8428fb61a42fc969b99f101c4737bdedf9bf036ff.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$",
      "worked_solution": "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}-0=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{4\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $3$ units down. Hence, $D=-3.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=\\frac{3}{4}+(-3)=-\\frac{9}{4}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{3x}{4})-3.\n$$",
